# Pacing_Design — Skeleton

## 1. Applies when
Narrative or horror genres where tension curves are designed deliberately (horror_narrative, linear_action_adventure).

## 2. This document owns
- Tension/relief curves per act and per chapter; intensity budgets.
- Segment rhythm targets (combat/exploration/story mix ratios).
- Quiet-time and downtime design intent.

## 3. This document does NOT own
- Encounter specifics — Encounter / Enemy design.
- Story content — Bible / Script.
- Level spatialization of pacing beats — Level_Design.

## 4. Recommended chapter structure
1. Curve philosophy / 2. Act curves / 3. Beat vocabulary / 4. Intensity budgets / 5. Downtime rules / 6. Measurement plan / 7. Open questions

## 5. Common boundaries
- Pacing vs Level: pacing defines *when* intensity happens; Level defines *where*.
- Pacing vs Mission: pacing constrains mission ordering; missions own their internal beats.

## 6. Common audit rules
- Every act has a declared curve; every set-piece maps to a curve peak.
- Downtime exists after every declared peak (no stacked peaks without relief).
